WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.75
This is journey twenty one of Hewn, the software factory: manage Owner and Viewer

00:00:06.75 --> 00:00:07.20
access.

00:00:07.20 --> 00:00:11.74
Only GitHub logins on the allow list can sign in, and each has a role.

00:00:11.74 --> 00:00:15.04
Owners give direction and stop work; Viewers watch.

00:00:15.04 --> 00:00:19.58
This video walks through how it was built: the allow list in Settings,

00:00:19.58 --> 00:00:24.89
how removing a login signs it out at once, the checks that keep Viewers read-only,

00:00:24.89 --> 00:00:25.80
and the tests.

00:00:26.95 --> 00:00:32.21
In Settings, an Owner adds a GitHub login as Owner or Viewer, or removes one,

00:00:32.21 --> 00:00:34.74
and a Viewer sees the list read-only.

00:00:34.74 --> 00:00:39.31
A Viewer gets four oh three from every endpoint that changes state,

00:00:39.31 --> 00:00:41.09
whatever the client shows.

00:00:41.09 --> 00:00:46.55
And a project the user can't see answers not found, with no hint that it exists.

00:00:47.70 --> 00:00:48.93
Here is the flow.

00:00:48.93 --> 00:00:52.40
The Owner opens Add a GitHub login and confirms.

00:00:52.40 --> 00:00:55.72
The API checks the login isn't listed already,

00:00:55.72 --> 00:00:58.82
and asks GitHub whether the account exists.

00:00:58.82 --> 00:01:02.57
Only then is it added, and they can sign in at once.

00:01:02.92 --> 00:01:08.16
The handler refuses a login that's listed, malformed, or unknown to GitHub,

00:01:08.16 --> 00:01:10.81
each with an error on the login field.

00:01:10.81 --> 00:01:14.65
When GitHub can't say, for example when it rate limits,

00:01:14.65 --> 00:01:17.52
nothing is added and the dialog says why.

00:01:17.87 --> 00:01:19.74
Removing works the other way.

00:01:19.74 --> 00:01:25.03
The login leaves the allow list, and its sessions end on their next request: every

00:01:25.03 --> 00:01:30.33
request now checks that its login is still listed with the role it signed in with.

00:01:30.33 --> 00:01:35.30
The logins and roles are held in memory, so the check costs no database read.

00:01:35.30 --> 00:01:39.69
An Owner can't remove themselves, so the list always keeps an Owner.

00:01:39.69 --> 00:01:42.21
That is recorded as A D R twenty three.

00:01:43.36 --> 00:01:45.82
Settings shows the list to everyone.

00:01:45.82 --> 00:01:51.51
Only an Owner sees Add a GitHub login, and a Remove button on every login but their

00:01:51.51 --> 00:01:51.79
own.

00:01:51.79 --> 00:01:56.86
The sidebar hides Audit and New direction from Viewers, as the mocks show.

00:01:57.21 --> 00:02:00.03
Hiding a button isn't the protection, though.

00:02:00.03 --> 00:02:02.09
The server answers four oh three.

00:02:02.09 --> 00:02:06.16
Here is what it answers when a Viewer sends the requests by hand,

00:02:06.16 --> 00:02:08.47
and when a removed login tries again.

00:02:09.62 --> 00:02:15.41
Every project route answers a project the caller can't see exactly as one that never

00:02:15.41 --> 00:02:18.64
existed: four oh four, with the code not found.

00:02:18.64 --> 00:02:20.85
The hub refuses to join it, too.

00:02:22.00 --> 00:02:27.22
The backend tests came first, and failed with not found: there was no route yet.

00:02:27.22 --> 00:02:32.18
Nine now cover the list, adding, the field errors, GitHub failing, removing,

00:02:32.18 --> 00:02:32.96
and Viewers.

00:02:33.31 --> 00:02:36.18
Two more call every endpoint the API maps.

00:02:36.18 --> 00:02:41.64
One sends each state-changing request as a Viewer and expects four oh three; the

00:02:41.64 --> 00:02:47.30
other asks for every project route of a project nobody can see and expects the same

00:02:47.30 --> 00:02:47.99
not found.

00:02:47.99 --> 00:02:52.90
Both passed at once, because earlier journeys already enforce the rules,

00:02:52.90 --> 00:02:55.90
and now any new endpoint without them fails.

00:02:56.25 --> 00:03:01.79
All green, the backend has two hundred and seventy six tests and the frontend three

00:03:01.79 --> 00:03:02.79
new view tests.

00:03:02.79 --> 00:03:06.86
End to end, there are fifteen new tests: seven for behaviour,

00:03:06.86 --> 00:03:11.59
three visual comparisons with the mocks, and five accessibility checks.

00:03:12.74 --> 00:03:15.39
Here the Owner adds nadia-k as a Viewer.

00:03:15.39 --> 00:03:18.16
She's listed at once, never signed in yet.

00:03:18.51 --> 00:03:20.38
She signs in through GitHub.

00:03:20.38 --> 00:03:24.72
There's no New direction and no Audit, and Settings is read-only.

00:03:24.72 --> 00:03:27.32
By hand, her changes get four oh three.

00:03:27.67 --> 00:03:29.07
The Owner removes her.

00:03:29.07 --> 00:03:32.56
Her session ends on its next request, with four oh one.

00:03:32.91 --> 00:03:36.72
A login already on the list, and one GitHub couldn't confirm,

00:03:36.72 --> 00:03:39.03
each say what happened in the dialog.

00:03:39.38 --> 00:03:42.58
The list reads the same in dark mode, and on a phone.

00:03:43.73 --> 00:03:45.94
Journey twenty one is complete.

00:03:45.94 --> 00:03:51.49
Owners add and remove GitHub logins as Owner or Viewer, confirmed with GitHub.

00:03:51.49 --> 00:03:55.04
A removed login is signed out on its next request.

00:03:55.04 --> 00:03:58.03
Viewers get four oh three on every change,

00:03:58.03 --> 00:04:01.52
and a project nobody can see is simply not found.

00:04:01.52 --> 00:04:03.65
Next comes journey twenty two.
